WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
Frameworks: when to adopt one.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:07.747
Two open-source frameworks are widely used, and both are worth reading even if you don’t adopt them.

3
00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:24.731
NVIDIA NeMo Guardrails organizes checks as rails that run at different stages (input, retrieval, dialog, execution, and output), configured with YAML plus Colang, a small language for conversational flows, and extended with Python actions.

4
00:00:25.011 --> 00:00:33.139
Its rails can call an LLM to self-check inputs and outputs, which means each such rail adds a model call you should count.

5
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:35.424
Guardrails AI (Apache 2.

6
00:00:35.704 --> 00:00:48.163
0) wraps calls in a Guard that runs validators on inputs or outputs, with prebuilt validators installed individually from its Hub; it also handles structured output from a Pydantic model.

7
00:00:48.443 --> 00:00:58.469
At the time of writing its README notes that hosted remote inferencing for validators is being discontinued, a reminder to check the status of anything you depend on.

8
00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:08.776
A framework earns its place when you have many policies across many routes, non-engineers who need to edit policies, or dialog flows that need explicit control.

9
00:01:09.056 --> 00:01:15.371
For one or two routes, the 150 lines you will write in the lab are easier to read, test, and measure.

10
00:01:15.651 --> 00:01:25.208
Either way, the measurement discipline is the same: a labeled red-team set, block rate, false-positive rate, and latency per guard, rerun on every change.
